      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the route. The laptop asks company DNS for go.corp.internal, and connects to a node on port 443."
        },
        {
          "text": "Traefik decrypts the request, and matches it against the Ingress rules."
        },
        {
          "text": "The rule's Service leads to an EndpointSlice: the list of ready pods. One pod takes the request, and asks Postgres where jira points."
        },
        {
          "text": "Then the answer goes back the same way. Eight hops. Let's take them one at a time.",
          "pauseAfter": 0.4
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dns",
      "type": "terminal",
      "title": "laptop",
      "steps": [
        {
          "at": 0,
          "command": "dig +short go.corp.internal",
          "output": [
            "10.0.0.11",
            "10.0.0.21",
            "10.0.0.22"
          ]
        },
        {
          "at": 1,
          "command": "curl -sv https://go.corp.internal/jira/1234 2>&1 | grep Connected",
          "output": [
            "* Connected to go.corp.internal (10.0.0.22) port 443"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Hop one: the name. Company DNS answers with all three node addresses, from Episode 5."
        },
        {
          "text": "This time curl chose the third address: agent 2, on port 443."
        },
        {
          "text": "DNS doesn't check whether a node is up. If one is down, most clients try the next address, but only after a wait.",
          "pauseAfter": 0.4
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "svclb",
      "type": "terminal",
      "title": "~/k8s-videos",
      "steps": [
        {
          "at": 0,
          "command": "kubectl get po -n kube-system -o wide | awk '/^svclb|^traefik/{print $1,$7}'",
          "output": [
            "svclb-traefik-2b4e1c7a-6hx2r k3s-server-1",
            "svclb-traefik-2b4e1c7a-qw8lm k3s-agent-1",
            "svclb-traefik-2b4e1c7a-zk5vn k3s-agent-2",
            "traefik-c98fdf6fb-kq7xn k3s-server-1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Hop two: the node. Every node runs a small ServiceLB pod, and on agent 2, that pod holds port 443."
        },
        {
          "text": "It forwards the connection to the Traefik Service, and the rules that kube-proxy set up pick a Traefik pod. There's only one, and it runs on the server."
        },
        {
          "text": "So the request has already crossed from one machine to another. That's normal, and we'll see how next.",
          "pauseAfter": 0.4
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "tls",
      "type": "terminal",
      "title": "~/k8s-videos",
      "steps": [
        {
          "at": 0,
          "command": "kubectl describe ingress golinks -n golinks | grep -A7 TLS",
          "output": [
            "TLS:",
            "  golinks-tls terminates go.corp.internal",
            "Rules:",
            "  Host              Path  Backends",
            "  ----              ----  --------",
            "  go.corp.internal",
            "                    /   golinks:http (10.42.1.21:8080,10.42.2.14:8080)"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Hop three is TLS. Traefik terminates it here, using the certificate in the Secret golinks-tls, which cert-manager keeps fresh."
        },
        {
          "text": "From here to the pod, the request is plain HTTP on the pod network. Inside a cluster that's common. A service mesh can encrypt that leg too."
        },
        {
          "text": "Hop four is the rule. For the host go.corp.internal and the path slash, it sends requests to the golinks Service, on its http port.",
          "pauseAfter": 0.4
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "eps",
      "type": "terminal",
      "title": "~/k8s-videos",
      "steps": [
        {
          "at": 0,
          "command": "kubectl get endpointslices -n golinks",
          "output": [
            "NAME             ADDRESSTYPE   PORTS   ENDPOINTS               AGE",
            "golinks-x7k2m    IPv4          8080    10.42.1.21,10.42.2.14   5h",
            "postgres-8hq4d   IPv4          5432    10.42.1.12              5h"
          ]
        },
        {
          "at": 2,
          "command": "kubectl get pods -n golinks -o wide | awk '{print $1,$6,$7}' | column -t",
          "output": [
            "NAME                      IP          NODE",
            "golinks-6c9d7f8b5c-n4hzx  10.42.2.14  k3s-agent-2",
            "golinks-6c9d7f8b5c-r7tqw  10.42.1.21  k3s-agent-1",
            "postgres-0                10.42.1.12  k3s-agent-1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "lines": [
        {
          "text": "Hop five is the EndpointSlice. Traefik doesn't send traffic to the Service's own address. It reads the EndpointSlice and picks a pod itself, taking turns."
        },
        {
          "text": "Only ready pods are listed. That's the readiness probe from Episode 6, deciding who gets traffic."
        },
        {
          "text": "And here's where each pod runs. Both golinks pods are ready, one on each agent."
        },
        {
          "text": "Postgres has its own slice, with its one pod.",
          "pauseAfter": 0.4
        }
      ]
    },
